49	/**
50	 * This is the main class for using Gson. Gson is typically used by first constructing a {@code
51	 * Gson} instance and then invoking {@link #toJson(Object)} or {@link #fromJson(String, Class)}
52	 * methods on it. {@code Gson} instances are thread-safe so you can reuse them freely across
53	 * multiple threads.
54	 *
55	 * <p>You can create a {@code Gson} instance by invoking {@link #Gson() new Gson()} if the default
56	 * configuration is all you need. You can also use {@link GsonBuilder} to build a {@code Gson}
57	 * instance with various configuration options such as versioning support, pretty printing, and
58	 * custom serialization and deserialization logic.
59	 *
60	 * <p>Gson is based on the concept of {@link TypeAdapter}s which contain the actual logic for
61	 * serializing and deserializing a Java type. For more advanced use cases {@link TypeAdapterFactory}
62	 * can be used which allows inspecting the Java type at runtime and providing a {@code TypeAdapter}
63	 * for it. {@code TypeAdapterFactory} also supports delegating to adapters for other types, which is
64	 * useful when handling types which are themselves composed of multiple types or when extending an
65	 * existing adapter.<br>
66	 * The interfaces {@link JsonSerializer} and {@link JsonDeserializer} allow processing JSON data as
67	 * tree structures; this can make their usage easier than {@code TypeAdapter}, but they are not as
68	 * efficient.
69	 *
70	 * <p>Gson provides built-in type adapters for many standard Java types such as {@code List}, {@code
71	 * Integer} or {@code String}. For unknown classes it uses reflection to inspect the fields of the
72	 * class and uses them for the JSON structure.
73	 *
74	 * <p>When serializing or deserializing a type, {@code Gson} instances cache the {@code TypeAdapter}
75	 * they obtained from a {@code TypeAdapterFactory} for that type. It can therefore improve
76	 * performance to reuse a single {@code Gson} instance (for example by storing it in a {@code static
77	 * final} field), especially when relying on the built-in reflection-based adapter or when using
78	 * custom type adapter factories.
79	 *
80	 * <p>Here is an example of how Gson is used for a simple Class:
81	 *
82	 * <pre>
83	 * Gson gson = new Gson(); // Or use new GsonBuilder().create();
84	 * MyType target = new MyType();
85	 * String json = gson.toJson(target); // serializes target to JSON
86	 * MyType target2 = gson.fromJson(json, MyType.class); // deserializes json into target2
87	 * </pre>
88	 *
89	 * <p>If the type of the object that you are converting is a {@code ParameterizedType} (i.e. has at
90	 * least one type argument, for example {@code List<MyType>}) then for deserialization you must use
91	 * a {@code fromJson} method with {@link Type} or {@link TypeToken} parameter to specify the
92	 * parameterized type. For serialization specifying a {@code Type} or {@code TypeToken} is optional,
93	 * otherwise Gson will use the runtime type of the object. {@link TypeToken} is a class provided by
94	 * Gson which helps creating parameterized types. Here is an example showing how this can be done:
95	 *
96	 * <pre>
97	 * TypeToken&lt;List&lt;MyType&gt;&gt; listType = new TypeToken&lt;List&lt;MyType&gt;&gt;() {};
98	 * List&lt;MyType&gt; target = new LinkedList&lt;MyType&gt;();
99	 * target.add(new MyType(1, "abc"));
100	 *
101	 * Gson gson = new Gson();
102	 * // For serialization you normally do not have to specify the type, Gson will use
103	 * // the runtime type of the objects, however you can also specify it explicitly
104	 * String json = gson.toJson(target, listType.getType());
105	 *
106	 * // But for deserialization you have to specify the type
107	 * List&lt;MyType&gt; target2 = gson.fromJson(json, listType);
108	 * </pre>
109	 *
110	 * <p>See the <a href="https://github.com/google/gson/blob/main/UserGuide.md">Gson User Guide</a>
111	 * for a more complete set of examples.
112	 *
113	 * <h2 id="default-lenient">JSON Strictness handling</h2>
114	 *
115	 * For legacy reasons most of the {@code Gson} methods allow JSON data which does not comply with
116	 * the JSON specification when no explicit {@linkplain Strictness strictness} is set (the default).
117	 * To specify the strictness of a {@code Gson} instance, you should set it through {@link
118	 * GsonBuilder#setStrictness(Strictness)}.
119	 *
120	 * <p>For older Gson versions, which don't have the strictness mode API, the following workarounds
121	 * can be used:
122	 *
123	 * <h3>Serialization</h3>
124	 *
125	 * <ol>
126	 *   <li>Use {@link #getAdapter(Class)} to obtain the adapter for the type to be serialized
127	 *   <li>When using an existing {@code JsonWriter}, manually apply the writer settings of this
128	 *       {@code Gson} instance listed by {@link #newJsonWriter(Writer)}.<br>
129	 *       Otherwise, when not using an existing {@code JsonWriter}, use {@link
130	 *       #newJsonWriter(Writer)} to construct one.
131	 *   <li>Call {@link TypeAdapter#write(JsonWriter, Object)}
132	 * </ol>
133	 *
134	 * <h3>Deserialization</h3>
135	 *
136	 * <ol>
137	 *   <li>Use {@link #getAdapter(Class)} to obtain the adapter for the type to be deserialized
138	 *   <li>When using an existing {@code JsonReader}, manually apply the reader settings of this
139	 *       {@code Gson} instance listed by {@link #newJsonReader(Reader)}.<br>
140	 *       Otherwise, when not using an existing {@code JsonReader}, use {@link
141	 *       #newJsonReader(Reader)} to construct one.
142	 *   <li>Call {@link TypeAdapter#read(JsonReader)}
143	 *   <li>Call {@link JsonReader#peek()} and verify that the result is {@link JsonToken#END_DOCUMENT}
144	 *       to make sure there is no trailing data
145	 * </ol>
146	 *
147	 * Note that the {@code JsonReader} created this way is only 'legacy strict', it mostly adheres to
148	 * the JSON specification but allows small deviations. See {@link
149	 * JsonReader#setStrictness(Strictness)} for details.
150	 *
151	 * @see GsonBuilder
152	 * @author Inderjeet Singh
153	 * @author Joel Leitch
154	 * @author Jesse Wilson
155	 */
156	public final class Gson {
