204	  /**
205	   * Constructs a Gson object with default configuration. The default configuration has the
206	   * following settings:
207	   *
208	   * <ul>
209	   *   <li>The JSON generated by {@code toJson} methods is in compact representation. This means
210	   *       that all the unneeded white-space is removed. You can change this behavior with {@link
211	   *       GsonBuilder#setPrettyPrinting()}.
212	   *   <li>When the JSON generated contains more than one line, the kind of newline and indent to
213	   *       use can be configured with {@link GsonBuilder#setFormattingStyle(FormattingStyle)}.
214	   *   <li>The generated JSON omits all the fields that are null. Note that nulls in arrays are kept
215	   *       as is since an array is an ordered list. Moreover, if a field is not null, but its
216	   *       generated JSON is empty, the field is kept. You can configure Gson to serialize null
217	   *       values by setting {@link GsonBuilder#serializeNulls()}.
218	   *   <li>Gson provides default serialization and deserialization for Enums, {@link Map}, {@link
219	   *       java.net.URL}, {@link java.net.URI}, {@link java.util.Locale}, {@link java.util.Date},
220	   *       {@link java.math.BigDecimal}, and {@link java.math.BigInteger} classes. If you would
221	   *       prefer to change the default representation, you can do so by registering a type adapter
222	   *       through {@link GsonBuilder#registerTypeAdapter(Type, Object)}.
223	   *   <li>The default Date format is same as {@link java.text.DateFormat#DEFAULT}. This format
224	   *       ignores the millisecond portion of the date during serialization. You can change this by
225	   *       invoking {@link GsonBuilder#setDateFormat(int, int)} or {@link
226	   *       GsonBuilder#setDateFormat(String)}.
227	   *   <li>By default, Gson ignores the {@link com.google.gson.annotations.Expose} annotation. You
228	   *       can enable Gson to serialize/deserialize only those fields marked with this annotation
229	   *       through {@link GsonBuilder#excludeFieldsWithoutExposeAnnotation()}.
230	   *   <li>By default, Gson ignores the {@link com.google.gson.annotations.Since} annotation. You
231	   *       can enable Gson to use this annotation through {@link GsonBuilder#setVersion(double)}.
232	   *   <li>The default field naming policy for the output JSON is same as in Java. So, a Java class
233	   *       field {@code versionNumber} will be output as {@code "versionNumber"} in JSON. The same
234	   *       rules are applied for mapping incoming JSON to the Java classes. You can change this
235	   *       policy through {@link GsonBuilder#setFieldNamingPolicy(FieldNamingPolicy)}.
236	   *   <li>By default, Gson excludes {@code transient} or {@code static} fields from consideration
237	   *       for serialization and deserialization. You can change this behavior through {@link
238	   *       GsonBuilder#excludeFieldsWithModifiers(int...)}.
239	   *   <li>No explicit strictness is set. You can change this by calling {@link
240	   *       GsonBuilder#setStrictness(Strictness)}.
241	   * </ul>
242	   */
243	  public Gson() {
244	    this(GsonBuilder.DEFAULT);
245	  }
